#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f05c1cc-9601-58e1-b22b-0c9137e0eba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291871} {:answer/id #uuid "efce043f-c81a-5694-8512-ac98dc0580d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291872} {:answer/id #uuid "71b9adf1-11c1-506e-a9c7-fc16dc13129e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291873} {:answer/id #uuid "3b36ea2c-51c5-55cb-9634-5f1fc7cf3ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291874} {:answer/id #uuid "89c5a5f4-5603-5cf5-ba87-8d510cdf568f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b9adf1-11c1-506e-a9c7-fc16dc13129e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291873} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8acd680-1834-52d3-a1e2-a9009635a9d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5b8f26e-0fd6-5a4b-9564-e6d55b96cd5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88689" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
